From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 06:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] riscv64: size the mmap range by the window mmap
 allocates from
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: The previous patch pins a sealed riscv64 binary to the Sv48 layout: it
answers GetMaxVirtualAddress() with 0x7fffffffffff and places the shadow at
0x7fff8000, and __asan_init refuses to run at all on a kernel that puts the
stack above that.  sanitizer_platform.h was left saying something else about
the same address space -- SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE for riscv64 is 1ULL << 56 --
so the binary asserts a 47-bit address space for its shadow and reserves a
bytemap for a 56-bit one.

What that costs is .bss, and it is not a disk cost: .bss is zero bytes in the
file and `memsz` in a LOAD segment, so it is paid by anything that ingests LOAD
segments rather than file contents.  SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE is read by five
allocator configurations as kSpaceSize; on Linux/riscv64 exactly one of them is
in an ASan binary, because SANITIZER_CAN_USE_ALLOCATOR64 is 1 there and ASan's
own primary is a SizeClassAllocator64 over a named 128G region.  The one left is
the internal allocator's SizeClassAllocator32, where kSpaceSize picks the
ByteMap that maps a 1MB region to its size class:

  kTwoLevelByteMapSize1 = (kSpaceSize >> 20) >> 12

  1ULL << 56  ->  2^24 entries  ->  map1_ is 128MiB
  1ULL << 47  ->  2^15 entries  ->  map1_ is 256KiB

Measured, riscv64, `int main(void){return 0;}` linked with asan-musl.cfg:
__sanitizer::internal_alloc_placeholder goes from 134,484,032 bytes to 528,448,
and the four LOAD segments' memsz from 145,190,120 (138.46MiB) to 11,234,544
(10.71MiB).  The same program linked with asan-musl-sealed.cfg gives the same
numbers, before and after, because the seal is a --defsym and this is a
compile-time constant.

That last clause is why this is not conditional on sealed mode, and it is worth
saying plainly: one libclang_rt.asan.a serves both modes, the ByteMap's size is
a template parameter, and a link-time symbol cannot choose a type.  So the
question is not "is 1ULL << 47 right for a sealed binary" but "is it right for
Linux/riscv64", and it is.  arch/riscv allocates an unhinted mmap from
DEFAULT_MAP_WINDOW = 1 << (MMAP_VA_BITS - 1) with MMAP_VA_BITS saturating at 48,
so the stack top is 0x800000000000 and no address a process is given without
asking for one reaches 1ULL << 47 -- on Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 alike.  1ULL << 56
is the width of the widest address Sv57's page tables can name, which is not the
same question.  The FIXME above the block says as much, and the two arms around
this one are the precedent: Fuchsia's riscv64 already writes 1ULL << 38 here,
and non-Apple aarch64 writes 1ULL << 48 on a Linux that can run a 52-bit VA.

The allocator is not narrowed by this.  Its regions come from
MmapAlignedOrDieOnFatalError with no hint, which is exactly what the window
bounds; pointers above kSpaceSize are still handled, because PointerIsMine
range-checks before it indexes and routes them to the secondary; and a region
that did land above the bound would fail the CHECK_LT in ComputeRegionId, which
is a loud abort at map time and not a mismapped byte.  What is narrowed is a
riscv64 Linux kernel that hands out unhinted mappings above 1 << 47, which is
also a kernel a sealed binary already refuses and an unsealed one has no shadow
for.

The two constants are held equal by a static_assert in sanitizer_sealed.h rather
than by a comment, because a comment is what let them drift apart the first
time.
---
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform.h | 29 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h   | 20 +++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 49 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform.h
index 5796640..1953fca 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform.h
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform.h
@@ -321,6 +321,35 @@
 // assumed to be some constant integer.
 #  if SANITIZER_FUCHSIA
 #    define SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE (1ULL << 38)
+#  elif SANITIZER_LINUX
+// Linux/riscv64 is the case the FIXME above is answered for here, and the
+// answer is the width of the window mmap picks from rather than the width of
+// the hardware's addresses.  RISC-V has Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57, so the widest
+// address a page table can name is 56 bits and 1ULL << 56 is what the fallback
+// below writes down; but arch/riscv caps the window an unhinted mmap allocates
+// from at DEFAULT_MAP_WINDOW = 1 << (MMAP_VA_BITS - 1), and MMAP_VA_BITS
+// saturates at 48 -- so the stack top is 0x800000000000 and the top address a
+// process is given without asking for one is 0x7fffffffffff, on Sv48 and Sv57
+// alike.  That is the same fact sealed init pins, and the two are held equal by
+// a static_assert in sanitizer_sealed.h.
+//
+// What reads this on Linux/riscv64 is one allocator: SANITIZER_CAN_USE_ALLOCATOR64
+// is 1 here, so ASan's own primary is SizeClassAllocator64 over a named 128G
+// region and does not consult it, and the reader left is the internal
+// allocator's SizeClassAllocator32, where kSpaceSize sizes the first level of
+// the bytemap that maps a region address to its size class.  1ULL << 56 makes
+// that array 2^24 entries, 128MiB of .bss in every riscv64 binary that links a
+// sanitizer, against 256KiB here.  Nothing is unmapped by the change: the
+// allocator's regions come from an unhinted mmap, which is exactly what the
+// window bounds, and a region that somehow landed above it would fail the
+// CHECK_LT in ComputeRegionId rather than be silently mismapped.
+//
+// The non-Apple aarch64 case below is the precedent: Linux/aarch64 can run a
+// 52-bit VA and the value written there is 1ULL << 48, for this reason.  So is
+// the SANITIZER_FUCHSIA arm just above, which is 1ULL << 38 on the same
+// hardware this fallback calls 56-bit, because Fuchsia's riscv64 user address
+// space is a known width.
+#    define SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE (1ULL << 47)
 #  else
 #    define SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE FIRST_32_SECOND_64(1ULL << 32, 1ULL << 56)
 #  endif
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h
index 77858ae..385a4dc 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h
@@ -113,6 +113,26 @@ inline bool SealedInit() { return &__sanitizer_sealed_init != nullptr; }
 // the mapping table above; nothing else here depends on which it is.
 const uptr kSealedMaxUserVirtualAddress = 0x7fffffffffffULL;
 const uptr kSealedShadowOffset = 0x7fff8000ULL;
+
+// The pin above is one statement about the address space, and
+// SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE is the same statement made in sanitizer_platform.h,
+// where it sizes the first level of the bytemap the internal allocator uses to
+// find a region's size class.  They were allowed to disagree once -- the pin
+// said Sv48 and the range size said 56 bits -- and the cost of that was not
+// wrongness but 128MiB of .bss where 256KiB was called for.  A header that
+// sanitizer_platform.h could include would be the tidier fix; it includes
+// nothing, by design, so this is what holds the two together: move the pin and
+// this stops the build until the other moves with it.
+//
+// It is an equality and not just the soundness bound (>=), deliberately.  The
+// bound is what makes the allocator correct, and a range size larger than the
+// pin would satisfy it; but a binary that pins Sv48 and then reserves a bytemap
+// for an address space it has just said it will refuse to run in is carrying
+// the cost of a generality the seal has already spent.
+static_assert(kSealedMaxUserVirtualAddress == SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE - 1,
+              "sealed riscv64 pins the top user address, and "
+              "SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE names the same address space; they "
+              "have to be the same number");
 #endif
 
 // The frame the runtime's initialization started on, recorded by __asan_init
-- 
2.43.0

